      scope:'JANUARY 2026 · 10 STORIES',
      script:`Welcome to HiTeach Radio's January 2026 news digest — the month Taiwan's classrooms came back from winter break running, not walking, into the new semester.

Start with the biggest number of the month: HABOOK's 2025 year-in-review data. Across its platforms, students spent nearly 1.5 million hours in HiTeach-powered lessons last year — schools from Taoyuan's district-wide rollout to Penghu's small island classrooms all showing the same pattern: adoption is up, but more importantly, engagement is deeper. Teachers aren't just turning the system on; they're building whole lessons around it.

That deeper engagement showed up everywhere this month. At Longan Elementary in Taoyuan, fourth-graders squared off in a "Digital Reading Tournament" — a full-grade reading duel run through HiTeach's contest mode, with Principal Chien Shan-ying leading the charge to prove AI tools can make reading more contagious, not less. Over at Zhongxing Elementary — a hundred-year-old school tucked into the hills of Daxi — Principal Tseng Ching-yi is running a gentler version of the same transformation, calling her century-old campus a "digital Provence" as it quietly grows new tech roots.

On the academic side, Wang Yu at Taipei's University-affiliated Elementary ran a full public lesson combining generative AI with mind-mapping for a social-studies unit on local landmarks — and had it analyzed afterward by AI Sokrates, HABOOK's classroom-analytics tool, which gave the lesson a clean sweep on its T, P and E indicators. In Nantou, Sanguang Junior High skipped the worksheet entirely and ran a hundred-student math tournament through HiTeach's Challenge Mode instead.

Two product notes worth flagging. HiTA — the teacher's companion app — picked up a round of updates this month: wireless document-camera sharing, a live peek behind student answers, and remote PowerPoint control, all aimed at the same goal HiTeach has been chasing all year — getting teachers out from behind the podium and walking the room. And a teacher nicknamed "Ah-Chi" published a workflow for using AI to build classroom board games from a lesson in about three minutes flat, which is either an efficiency story or a rebellion against Sunday-night lesson planning, depending how you look at it.

Smaller items from the month: HiTeach's "Smart Grading" module got a four-technique explainer — star ratings, multi-round voting, peer review, and peer-review-plus-AI; the HiTeach teacher community kicked off its January challenge focused on AI-assisted grading; and over in Miaoli, English teachers spent part of their winter break in a workshop with teacher Caroline Liu, working AI tools into listening, speaking, reading and writing instruction before the spring term even started.

That's January — ten stories, one clear theme: less time on the mechanics of teaching, more time actually looking at what students are thinking. See you in February.`,
