      title:'February 2026',
      scope:'FEBRUARY 2026 · 7 STORIES',
      script:`February in Taiwan means one thing on the school calendar: the short run-up to spring semester, and this month HABOOK spent it sharpening tools rather than launching anything flashy.

The headline product news was HiTeach Campus Edition — a two-minute explainer video positioning HiTeach not as something an individual teacher opts into, but as a whole-school rollout, the difference between one classroom going digital and every classroom moving together. It's aimed squarely at principals and IT coordinators rather than individual teachers, and it previews a theme we'll hear a lot more of later in the year: technology leadership as the real bottleneck in digital transformation, not the technology itself.

If you want proof that bottleneck can be solved, February gave you three teachers doing it. HABOOK packaged fifteen minutes of highlight reels from Wang Yu, Chen Bo-ren and Zhang Wenqiu — three teachers who between them cover math, language arts, and pure grading speed. Wang Yu's AI can catch a "copying error" hidden in messy handwriting; Chen Bo-ren has what the write-up only half-jokingly calls an "AI incantation" for sentence-writing exercises; and Zhang Wenqiu marked seventeen assignments in what the video implies is close to real time.

There's data behind the marking-speed story, too. A separate piece this month reported that pairing HiTeach's mastery-learning mode with pre-class previewing pushed measured learning gains up by 1.1 to 1.4 standard deviations — the kind of number that's easy to skim past, but represents a genuinely large effect size for a classroom intervention. It's built on the same three-part framework — starting point, grouped learning, consolidation — that HABOOK's research team formally published toward the end of the month, and that we cover in full in this show's Research Spotlight channel.

Internationally, HABOOK crossed a continent: HiTeach appeared at the Education Experts Oman Conference in Muscat, presented alongside Middle East partner Dr. Huang Li-ling — one data point in what turns out to be a running thread through the year, HiTeach showing up further and further from Taiwan.

And in smaller housekeeping: HABOOK opened a three-minute user survey asking teachers what's working and what isn't, with prizes for anyone who fills it in, and the teacher community's February–March challenge focused on stretching AI skills further — auto-grading, auto-summarizing, and AI-built mind maps.

Not a loud month, but a load-bearing one — most of what February quietly set up is what the rest of the year builds on.`,
