      title:'April 2026',
      scope:'APRIL 2026 · 9 STORIES',
      script:`April was HABOOK's most international month of the year so far — three separate delegations, on three different continents, inside three weeks.

Start close to home with two teachers earning the country's top classroom honor. Wu Nianzhou — whose work in Tainan we mentioned back in March — added the 2026 Tainan SUPER Teacher Award to his record. A week later, Wen Yingmei at Miaoli's Datong Elementary picked up Miaoli County's own SUPER Teacher Award, capping more than twenty years in the classroom that already includes a Shi Duo Award and a place on the Education Innovation 100 list. Her stated philosophy, translated simply, is that love and respect are the best teaching method there is.

Then the passport stamps start. In Jordan, Al-Ahliyya Amman University — a decade into its partnership with HABOOK — reported startling adoption numbers after upgrading from HiTeach 3 to HiTeach 5: 91.5 percent of faculty actively using the system, averaging 17.8 real-time interactions per class session. In the Philippines, a SEAMEO INNOTECH-led delegation of education officials visited Taipei Municipal University of Education's affiliated elementary school to see Taiwan's AI-classroom approach firsthand, part of Taiwan's "HiEDU" smart-content initiative. And closer to a language classroom than a boardroom, a delegation from the American Council on the Teaching of Foreign Languages — ACTFL — sat in on a bilingual class taught by Karen Chou at Shuangyuan Elementary, watching real-time feedback and game-based review play out in English and Mandarin for an international audience.

There was a domestic training story with the same shape: Chengdu Normal University in China rolled out HiTeach and AI Sokrates as the backbone of a "teach, practice, research, assess" integrated model for training future teachers — one answer to the broader policy question of how you actually measure a trainee teacher's digital-teaching competence, not just their theory.

On the publishing side, April closed with HABOOK releasing five core academic papers at once, packaged as a reference blueprint for how HiTeach and AI Sokrates map onto three of Taiwan's Ministry of Education digital-learning policies — useful reading for any school leadership team trying to plan a rollout rather than improvise one.

And two smaller notes to close the month: a 200-student, three-day World Book Day reading event that used HiTeach's video-conferencing integration to connect classrooms across the island in real time, and the community's April challenge, this one focused on differentiated-instruction features specifically.

From a Jordanian university lecture hall to a Philippine delegation to an American language-teaching body — April was the month HiTeach's classroom story started sounding less like a Taiwan story and more like a global one.`,
