      title:'May 2026',
      scope:'MAY 2026 · 12 STORIES',
      script:`May was HABOOK putting its philosophy into writing — literally, in one case — while also throwing the biggest competitions of the year so far.

Start with the paperwork, because two publications this month matter more than they sound. First, a piece making the case for HiTeach AI as what it calls a genuinely "education-purpose AI" — safe by design, teacher-directed, built for classroom summarizing, peer review and targeted remediation rather than open-ended chat. Second, and more specifically timed: HABOOK's positioning lined up almost point for point with new US Department of Education technology guidance released the same month, which called for AI that stays centered on teaching, keeps teachers in charge of the safety envelope, and backs decisions with real data — three boxes HABOOK was quick to note HiTeach already checks.

There's a real classroom example of "teacher-directed" AI writing this month too: a research team at Taipei Municipal University of Education's affiliated elementary — Feng Chia-yen, Xu Jing-yi and Lai Yi-hsuan — published their "WRITE" dual-process AI writing model, turning composition from a one-shot homework hand-in into something closer to a guided draft-and-revise cycle. Lai Yi-hsuan gets a second mention this month for a separate case study turning a "Three Kingdoms Detective" Chinese-literature unit into data-traceable inquiry learning, reviewed afterward through AI Sokrates.

Competition season was in full swing. Keelung ran its "Reading Wisdom King" contest — 41 school teams, 123 students, built around PIRLS-style dual literacy, print and digital. And at Central Taiwan University of Science and Technology, the nursing department's "TOP5 Nursing Licensing Exam Challenge" turned notoriously dry board-exam review into what the write-up calls an esports-style scramble — complete with a freshman team pulling off a surprise win in the tournament's first year.

Big-picture events filled out the rest of the month. HABOOK ran an online forum on turning the national "AI Talent Ark" policy into an actual school-level transformation roadmap, followed by a "five swords" explainer breaking HiTeach's AI toolkit into five concrete applications: sentence analysis, auto-grading, collaborative editing, a GPT helper, and an AI study companion. At the 2026 Basic Education Conference, HABOOK's Power Wu laid out the big-picture vision while research lead Liang Renkai took the stage himself to teach a live, AI-assisted math lesson in front of the academic crowd — theory and practice, back to back, same event.

And a personal note to close: Wu Nianzhou — already a SUPER Teacher award winner this spring — added Tainan's Shi Duo Award on top of it before May was out, making it two major honors in two months for one teacher's differentiated-teaching work.

May's pattern, in short: publish the theory, then go prove it works in a room full of people watching.`,
