      scope:'JUNE 2026 · 6 STORIES',
      script:`June was a quiet month by HiTeach Radio's standards — just six stories — but three of them landed on the very same day, so let's start there.

June 9th turned into something close to a HABOOK highlight reel. First, research lead Liang Renkai took a notoriously dry topic — probability — and turned it into a live public lesson for an education-ministry "AI Talent Ark" showcase, blending AI tools with project-based team learning to build intuition for uncertainty rather than just memorizing formulas. Second, HiTeach itself got updated the same day: version 5.5.0.0008 shipped with fifteen feature improvements, grouped around three goals — getting AI properly embedded in day-to-day teaching, cutting teacher workload, and sharpening the gamified side of the platform, including new anti-cheating protections on quizzes. And third, the 2026 Taipei Education Expo wrapped up that same week, with HABOOK's booth reportedly drawing lines all day around interactive games like digital whack-a-mole and a rocket-launch challenge — enough to earn a formal thank-you from Education Bureau Director Tang Chih-ming.

Earlier in the month, Liu Wenshang at Yunlin's Zhennan Elementary ran a public reading-comprehension lesson nicknamed the "Reading Lab: Cracking a Text with a Golden Fork" — using HiTeach's classroom tools alongside AI Sokrates' behavioral analytics to make a full class's thinking process visible in real time, rather than guessing at comprehension from a handful of raised hands. And a highlight-reel video went up featuring Zhang Wenqiu at Ruisui Elementary walking through five full Chinese-language lessons built around digital mind-maps, AI-generated questions, differentiated group work and AI-assisted essay grading.

The month closed with a Commonwealth Magazine feature on Wu Chunping, an associate professor at National Tainan University, whose work folding HiTeach and classroom-observation tools into teacher training is being held up as a model for making "teaching reflection" — normally a vague, hard-to-measure idea — into something that shows up in actual data.

Six stories, one live demonstration on June 9th of just about everything this show has been talking about all year — new features, a public lesson, and a room full of teachers and families lining up to try it themselves.`,
