      title:'July 2026',
      scope:'JULY 2026 · 8 STORIES',
      script:`July, in Taiwan, is summer break for students — and apparently peak conference season for everyone else. Eight stories this month, several of them visits.

Two separate visiting delegations came through HABOOK's door this month, both focused on the same underlying question: how do you actually train teachers to use AI well, not just tell them to? Taichung's Elementary Math Curriculum Guidance Group sat down with HABOOK founder Power Wu and research lead Liang Renkai for what's described as a wide-ranging exchange on AI-powered teaching and HiTeach's "five swords" toolkit applied specifically to math instruction. And the National Academy for Educational Research's Indigenous Education Research Center visited HABOOK's own headquarters to work through what a structured "AI-assisted teacher-training support system" should look like — trainable, assessable, and trackable, with a specific eye toward cultural relevance in Indigenous education.

HABOOK also formalized its policy-facing side this month, launching two dedicated hub pages — one for the national "AI Talent Ark" district rollout program, one for that same AI-assisted teacher-training system — aimed at giving education bureaus, county digital offices, schools and teacher-training institutions a single place to see how the pieces fit together.

On the recognition side: the AI-writing research team at Taipei Municipal University of Education's affiliated elementary — the same WRITE-model team from May — picked up a Taipei Smart Education Excellence Award in the general teaching-team category, this time specifically for their dual-process AI writing model in primary-school composition.

A few lighter notes: HiTeach's cloud-based mind-map and board-game features officially launched out of preview, with a giveaway campaign encouraging teachers to share what they'd built; HABOOK exhibited at the 66th National Science Fair inside ASUS's ExpertHub pavilion at National Central University, pairing HiTeach's interactive software with ASUS hardware; and a new integration story tied the Ministry of Education's "adaptive learning" platform together with HiTeach's group-learning mode, closing the loop between self-paced pre-class study and structured in-class collaboration.

The month closed on a leadership note: Commonwealth Magazine ran an AI-in-education forum where National Chengchi University professor Chang Yi-hua used a live HiTeach demonstration to walk roughly eighty elementary and junior-high principals through what "smart campus" leadership actually looks like in practice — less about buying software, more about governance, team collaboration, and AI oversight.

Eight stories, one recurring guest star: the word "system." July was the month HABOOK stopped talking about individual features and started talking about how the pieces — teachers, policy, training, leadership — fit together.`,
