{
  code:'FE', name:'Featured Educators', color:'#8C6FB0',
  blurb:'Twelve volumes through 成功案例・智慧名師 — 135 teachers and professors, undated, browsable anywhere.',
  episodes:[
    {
      title:'Volume 1',
      scope:'PAGE 1 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Welcome to Featured Educators — twelve short episodes, one for each page of HABOOK's "Smart Teacher" directory, working through more than a hundred educators who've built their teaching around HiTeach. No dates on these profiles; think of it less as news, more as a staff room you can drop into anywhere. This is Volume One.

We open with Lin Fangquan — "Tomato Teacher" — at Taoyuan's Dazhu Elementary, whose stated belief is that technology isn't a tool so much as a way to make sure more student voices get heard, not just the fastest hands in the room.

Two profiles cover the same teacher from two angles: Zhang Wenqiu at Taichung's Ruisui Elementary, whose math and Chinese-language lesson sequences both follow the same rule — games come first, as a way of building understanding, not as a reward for finishing early. In math, that means songs and board games before formal problem-solving; in Chinese, it means deciding, lesson by lesson, exactly where AI belongs across vocabulary, comprehension, and writing.

Chen Jiachuan at New Taipei's Dingpu Elementary reframes a question a lot of teachers are quietly asking: not "should I use technology," but "which tool, used how, actually helps this specific student." Professor Chen Xinxi takes the same skepticism to assessment itself, arguing — and demonstrating, by having workshop teachers sit through a HiTeach lesson as students — that a test should be the start of the next lesson, not the end of this one.

Liao Chunhong at Linkou Junior High solves a very specific problem: teaching the same subject across multiple classes with no time between periods to remember where each one left off, using AI to accelerate prep and HiTeach to carry that prep straight into the room. Lin Jiahua at Zhongxiao Junior High takes a first-aid unit — bandaging, bleeding control — and turns it into a self-made board game students want to replay, with AI feedback and a live leaderboard turning "fear of the test" into "eager to retry."

Dai Yujun, a department lead, tells a rollout story rather than a lesson story — starting with his own homeroom, adding live response and AI word-clouds to an ordinary slide deck, then scaling that same habit district-wide through structured classroom observation. Professor Chen Weilu at Central Taiwan University of Science and Technology has spent over a decade turning nursing licensing-exam prep — normally dead serious — into a running tournament, from early remote-clicker quizzes to today's "TOP5" competitions.

Chen Boren at Miaoli's Kezhuang Elementary runs a three-stage model — individual learning before class, differentiated collaboration during, critical reflection after — using AI not just to grade but to help students compare viewpoints and expand their own thinking. Zhang Yanrong at Kaohsiung's Xinxing Elementary calls HiTeach her "teammate" — a phrase that shows up a lot in this directory — for the way it lets her track a class's learning trail across challenges, mind-maps and parent-student reading records over time.

We close Volume One with Lin Jiaru at New Taipei's Haishan Elementary, whose honest starting point was being drawn in by "no lesson-planning required" — and who, one small experiment at a time, ended up building genuine student independence into her classroom instead.

Twelve teachers, one page of a very long directory. Eleven more volumes to go.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 1 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 2',
      scope:'PAGE 2 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Featured Educators, Volume Two — twelve more voices from HABOOK's teacher directory, this time mostly short, first-person reflections rather than full case studies.

Wu Xiangchun at Taichung's Neipu Elementary describes the shift in one image: a room that used to have passive recipients now has students "busy exploring and interacting" — no more bystanders. Chen Yijing brings a similar transformation to a much harder setting, an English classroom at a remote school in Miaoli's Donghe Elementary, flipping engagement through high-interaction teaching. Sun Qiongfeng at Pingtung's Zhongxiao Elementary puts it plainly: HiTeach cut her lesson-prep workload while raising classroom interactivity, turning teaching from one-way delivery into something with real back-and-forth.

Cai Yunzhen at Taoyuan's Dazhong Elementary offers the most quotable line in this batch: technology should make teaching easier on you, not add pressure — and a tech hiccup mid-lesson is just an opening for a student to become the helper. Fan Tingyu at Kaohsiung's Shouling Elementary builds English lessons around local food and real-world problem-solving, and has taken that same approach onto Instagram and Threads, coaching other teachers through the same AI tools she uses in her own room.

We hear again, briefly, from Chen Boren at Kezhuang Elementary — his three-stage model from Volume One, reduced to one line: every student gets a stage to show their thinking, not just the fastest hand in the room. Chen Yian at Hsinchu's Guangwu Junior High calls technology "the medium of learning design" itself, not just a support tool, with AI feedback turning his classroom into what he calls a genuine knowledge community.

Zhuang Shaoqun at Hsinchu's Taiping Elementary and Li Qinqi at Taipei's Zhiqing Elementary — nicknamed "Captain Surprise" by her students — both describe the same goal from different angles: using interactive software and data to keep curiosity and imagination, not just test scores, at the center of the room.

Liao Chunhong returns for a shorter callback to Volume One's profile — this time praising HiTeach simply as software that "puts a lot of separate tools into one," with real-time visibility into how every student is doing. Shi Kunlong at Tainan's Shengli Elementary describes turning a quiet Chinese-language class lively, with real-time diagnosis of learning gaps and true differentiated grouping.

We close Volume Two with Ye Yuechun at Hsinchu Girls' Senior High — a second-career teacher with a very different specialty: preserving Taiwanese and Hakka language, now certified in both at advanced level, alongside thirteen separate teaching credentials, working to keep mother-tongue education alive for the next generation.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 2 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 3',
      scope:'PAGE 3 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Three of Featured Educators moves briefly overseas before coming back to Taiwan's classrooms.

We open with a teacher known simply as "Teacher Abu," at Cupang Elementary School — the same Philippine school behind the "Smart Start" workshop we covered in our Events Roundup. His lesson opens with a "Think Tank Challenge," students brainstorming theme-related vocabulary before HiTeach walks them through three short stories from three different perspectives — real-time feedback turning theme analysis into what the write-up calls a genuinely immersive discussion.

Back in Taiwan: Wu Nianzhou at Tainan's Beishi Elementary — whose award season we covered across several News Digest episodes — gets the fuller version of his story here: a teacher who specializes in closing gaps between students in the same class, building custom Chinese and math tasks so every student, regardless of starting point, learns with confidence. Professor Chen Weilu, back for a second mention, puts his teaching philosophy in one memorable phrase: it's not enough to simply lecture well anymore — you need what he calls "soul-calling" power to keep a room's attention, and HiTeach's real-time interaction is how he does it.

Wen Yingmei at Miaoli's Datong Elementary — this show's April SUPER Teacher winner — is a veteran of both language teaching and learning-support instruction, working from the belief that every child holds untapped potential and that differentiated instruction is simply respect for that. Lin Shuping at Miaoli's Xinying Elementary leads her school's digital-teaching community, runs a public lesson every semester for visiting researchers, and holds certifications across multiple national digital-learning programs including Apple's own Learning Coach credential.

Wu Huizhen at Tainan's Kaiyuan Elementary describes technology as a catalyst for creativity and critical thinking as much as a differentiation tool. Two professors weigh in on the teacher-training side: Wu Jiadi at National Taipei University of Education uses HiTeach specifically to get trainee teachers comfortable with classroom technology before they ever lead their own room, prizing how quickly it can be picked up; and Lin Yimei, in National Chung Cheng University's communications department, highlights HiTeach's collaboration and GPT tools for running role-play and real-time reflection exercises with her own students.

We hear once more, briefly, from Lin Jiahua at Zhongxiao Junior High — this time on classroom management broadly, from attendance to motivation to transparent grading. Wang Fengyu at New Taipei's Leli Elementary and Xie Jiajin at Taichung's Qingshui Junior High both describe the same long-run goal in different words: building lessons lively enough, and habits sturdy enough — note-taking, error-logging, evidence-based reading — that students remember them years after graduation.

We close with Lai Huimin at Taipei Municipal University of Education's affiliated elementary, whose guiding rule is simple: a teacher who stops changing raises students who stop imagining.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 3 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 4',
      scope:'PAGE 4 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Four of Featured Educators — a run of twelve teachers, several from the same handful of Taipei schools, each finding a different entry point into the same platform.

Zhou Yumei — known to her bilingual first- and second-graders as "Ms. Chou" — teaches through hands-on, experiential lessons, and treats HiTeach mainly as a way to see her youngest students' learning more clearly. Li Yihui at Nangang Elementary remembers the old way of sharing a student's notebook — one camera, one student, one look at a time — and contrasts it with HiTeach's "work submission" feature, where an entire class can upload at once.

Gao Taizhu at Mingzhu Junior High has been turning abstract math symbols into images since 2017, a project that only got easier once every student had their own tablet in hand. Wang Huijun at Yilan's Beicheng Elementary, a longtime curriculum-development veteran, focuses on the practical question underneath all of this: how do you make new technology easy enough that teachers actually adopt it, and useful enough that students actually benefit?

Shi Xiumei at Taipei Municipal University of Education's affiliated elementary borrows her teaching philosophy from the educator Friedrich Froebel — "education is nothing but love and example" — and treats every student mistake as an opening for real classroom dialogue rather than a moment to move past. Chen Yanqi at Taoyuan's Ruiping Junior High makes a related point about reading the room: real-time response data means a teacher no longer has to guess, from a student's expression alone, whether a concept has actually landed.

Cai Yicen at Kaohsiung's Minzu Junior High had already built a classroom students loved before tablets arrived — structured lectures, group work, Socratic-style discussion — and describes the tablet less as a new method than as a way of making a good classroom even more compelling. Wang Yiwen at Taipei Municipal University's affiliated elementary frames her whole practice around one belief: real educational love means recognizing your responsibility in what a child actually needs, not just what's easiest to teach.

Zhang Xueli at National Southern Taiwan Science Park International Experimental School has cycled through nearly every teaching method on the list — full-text approaches, note-taking, mind-maps, worksheets, sticky-note discussions — before HiTeach gave her a platform that could hold all of it in one place. Zhang Xuewang at Tainan's Dacheng Elementary uses learning data specifically to spot each student's strengths and weak points, then differentiates from there. Li Peiyi at Taipei Municipal University's affiliated elementary remembers teaching with nothing but a projector and blackout curtains, and still sounds surprised, years later, at how much one electronic whiteboard and a remote clicker opened up. And Gan Mengjuan at Taichung's Yongan Elementary sums up the whole arc of her career in one line: from the document camera to the touchscreen to the tablet in every student's hands, each step just made the classroom a little more flexible, a little more interactive.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 4 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 5',
      scope:'PAGE 5 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Five of Featured Educators.

Lin Meijun's path ran through a decade of language teaching in Taoyuan before a posting to a tiny rural school in Chiayi's Budai taught her, in her words, that her prior experience "still wasn't enough" — leading her into a school-supported reading initiative that eventually opened up a much wider teaching practice than she'd started with. Huang Junqin, eight years into teaching high-school physics and doubling as IT coordinator at New Taipei's Linkou Senior High, leans on video and animation to make abstract physics concepts visible, recording lessons for students to rewatch and staying active in the district's mobile-learning and VR programs.

Lin Yuji has been pushing tech integration since becoming his school's IT lead back in 2002 — from wheeling around a single shared projector cart, to Wii-remote whiteboards, to HiTeach's very first "buzzer" version, all the way to today's touchscreens and one-tablet-per-student classrooms. A different teacher, also named Lin at Nangang Elementary, splits her energy between enriching lessons for advancing students and running remedial support for those falling behind, aiming to rebuild both competence and confidence.

Xu Yuqing at Taoyuan's Guangming Junior High — a school we've heard about before, in our Research Spotlight episodes — describes math lesson prep that can take hours to build a single forty-five-minute class, but says watching students genuinely engage makes clear that the real power of a smart classroom isn't novelty, it's how fast a teacher can check understanding and adjust. Cai Juling at Mingzhu Junior High works toward a classroom with, in her words, "no guests" — every student pulled into math from a different angle until it clicks. Ke Jingru at Chongqing Junior High moved from years of homeroom and Chinese-language teaching into library instruction, using digital tools and gamified reading challenges to reach students who'd otherwise disengage from reading altogether.

Chen Jiawen at Taoyuan's Nankan Elementary designs lessons around real-world problem-solving to build scientific literacy; Cai Yilan at Hsinchu's Bo'ai Elementary calls HiTeach's smart classroom simply the thing that "made reading class more fun." Xie Shumei at Chiayi's Yizhu Junior High carries an unusually decorated record — a second-place Smart Classroom Innovation Award, a county excellence award, and a spot as a practice-sharing teacher in the "Dream N" reading-education program. Cai Lanbin at Yilan's Gongzheng Elementary, trained at National Dong Hwa University's graduate institute of education, sits on textbook review and editorial committees while staying curious about Chinese-language curriculum design.

We close Volume Five with Huang Yushan at Taipei's Bailing Senior High's junior division, whose years of HiTeach experience across Chinese-language teaching and reading promotion lean hard on the platform's interaction, statistics, and quiz features to quickly read a class's understanding.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 5 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 6',
      scope:'PAGE 6 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Six of Featured Educators — a run heavy on reading and language teachers, with a detour into Chinese-teacher exchange programs.

Guo Donglin at Taipei's Chengzheng Junior High brings credentials in bilingual-education program design and a district bilingual-curriculum award. Xu Bihui at Nantou's Beimei Junior High has moved from homeroom teacher to academic dean, now active in the county's reading-comprehension mentor community. Xu Huijing at Tainan's Kaiyuan Elementary works within a cross-school reading community built around Professor Chen Xinxi's research team — the same researcher featured back in Volume One.

Zhang Fangxian — another "Tomato Teacher," this one at Yilan's Chenggong Elementary — describes her role less as delivering knowledge and more as walking alongside students while they find their own direction. Liang Shuping at Pingtung's Gaoshu Elementary applies Professor Chen's "Approach it, Understand it, Use it" literacy framework directly in her classroom, pairing team-based learning with HiTeach's smart-teaching tools.

A quick callback to Zhang Wenqiu, whose math and Chinese teaching we covered in depth back in Volume One — here summed up in her own words: she'd rather build "fun, useful" math grounded in real life than math confined to a textbook. Lin Xiuzhen at Chiayi's Guolu Elementary offers this collection's shortest, and maybe most honest, line: "each time, change a little; each time, learn a little more."

Xu Bishan at Taipei's Jinhua Elementary, a ten-year science teacher, also serves as a national instructor for Taiwan's adaptive-learning platform. Huang Jianting at Hsinchu's Dongxing Elementary centers Chinese and reading instruction on student thinking, crediting the "every student a tablet" policy with making that visible lesson to lesson, online or in person. Xie Yingzhen, also at Gaoshu Elementary, states her goal simply: to become the kind of teacher who genuinely moves people, by learning humbly from every colleague who came before her.

Gao Minhui at Kaohsiung's Qianjin Elementary uses HiTeach specifically with first-graders and students needing learning support, giving them a live, low-stakes way to show their progress and build confidence. And we close with Cai Mengyun — nicknamed "Little Gecko" — at Yilan's Zhulin Elementary, twenty years into teaching and sixteen years into a personal blog with nearly eight hundred posts, most of them on reading and writing instruction that other teachers and parents have followed for over a decade.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 6 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 7',
      scope:'PAGE 7 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Seven of Featured Educators.

Lin Mingjun at Kaohsiung's Qianjin Elementary has spent most of her career with the youngest grades, prioritizing critical thinking and classroom interaction, and credits HiTeach's data tools with making it easier to both gauge student ability and sharpen her own questioning. Li Xinyi at Nantou's Sanguang Junior High teaches Chinese in a remote district, where low engagement is the real daily challenge — her fix is simply keeping the room active and energetic enough that participation becomes automatic.

Two teachers from Taoyuan's Nankan Senior High round out a science-and-math thread: Lin Youyi and Huang Qiefeng, a homeroom teacher for the school's science-and-math track. Chen Mingli at Nangang Elementary combined tech-assisted teaching with reading comprehension work to build what she calls the "Hi~Oxford Reading Comprehension Model" — recognized with a Taipei outstanding-teacher award and a national innovation prize along the way.

Chen Wenyi, a department head at New Taipei's Beixin Elementary, has taught English for nearly two decades through situational, context-rich lessons, and pairs that approach with HiTeach's "Smart Steps" teaching sequence for a genuinely competency-based classroom. Xiao Xiuduan at Nantou's Sanguang Junior High uses features like "smart picking," second-chance answering and differentiated task delivery to let students take on challenges matched to their own level — while giving her a clearer, real-time read on where each student stands.

Huang Yanlun at Taoyuan's Ruiping Junior High splits class time between individual thinking and group collaboration, using the smart classroom's high engagement to catch problems early and scaffold in the moment. Jian Yimei at Taipei's Xinyi Junior High has spent years building reading culture from the classroom up to the whole school, and describes technology mainly as a way to help students show what they've learned more easily.

Li Yanxun at New Taipei's Shenkeng Elementary works across cross-disciplinary curriculum design informed by both classroom practice and academic theory; Dai Yude at Tainan's Zhongyi Elementary researches Taiwanese-language revival and local history alongside his own published poetry, and now focuses his classroom work on differentiated teaching and self-directed and project-based learning. And we close Volume Seven with Zhang Lingyun at Nangang Elementary, who folds global citizenship themes into her lessons, using bilingual reading materials, tablet-based team discussion, and individual digital learning portfolios to deepen the experience.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 7 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 8',
      scope:'PAGE 8 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Eight of Featured Educators opens with the person whose name has come up most often in this whole series: Power Wu, founder of the HABOOK Group and its TEAM Model / HiTeach brand, who built a research institute connecting smart-education researchers across more than ten countries — the umbrella under which just about everyone else in this show operates.

Xu Zhiyang at Nantou's Beimei Junior High describes his goal simply as wanting both himself and his students to learn with joy — using energy and humor to counter the low engagement common in his rural district. Chen Jiachuan, back for a fuller mention after Volume One, anchors her Chinese teaching in the belief that language ability underlies every other subject, building vocabulary and writing skill through newspaper-based reading education.

Zhou Shuhui at Taoyuan Elementary has spent over a decade in reading and writing instruction, using question-driven teaching and team-based learning to keep her classroom lively. Lin Peiyu at Taoyuan's Guangming Junior High teaches English for real use, not just tests — recruiting a foreign teacher to record video for a wayfinding unit, then watching reluctant students turn into the eager ones giving directions.

Cai Yiliang at Taoyuan's Jingguo Senior High builds geography lessons around map literacy, moving students from small-group discussion toward evidence-based argument — from memorized answers to actual understanding. Huang Xinhui at New Taipei's private Gezhi Senior High puts the same idea in a single line: a good modern teacher isn't just someone who can lecture, but someone who gets students to open their eyes and ears, and take ownership of their own learning.

Zhang Shunliang, a veteran Taoyuan math teacher, keeps exploring new materials and methods to spark curiosity about mathematical inquiry. Lian Ziting at Taoyuan's Yixing Elementary teaches English while pursuing her own graduate studies in childhood English education — someone actively pushing past her own comfort zone. Xu Huiling at Taipei's Zhiqing Elementary specializes in smart reading instruction and is a repeat winner across both teaching-innovation and research competitions.

Wang Yu, briefly mentioned back in our January News Digest, gets fuller billing here: twenty years of HiTeach classroom research distilled into what she calls the "thinking golden triangle" — the belief that a teacher first has to see how students are actually thinking before that thinking can expand, transform, and be applied elsewhere. We close with Yang Yating at Taoyuan's Dayou Junior High — the same school from our Research Spotlight episodes — whose geography lessons are built entirely around letting students use what they learn to solve real problems, turning her smart classroom into what she calls a genuine exploration.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 8 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 9',
      scope:'PAGE 9 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Nine of Featured Educators.

Xu Dawei at New Taipei's Beixin Elementary — whose live public math lesson we covered back in our March News Digest — describes his motivation plainly: he didn't want to just be "a regular elementary teacher," so he sought out every training and workshop he could find to make his classroom genuinely engaging. Lin Xinmei at Taipei's Shuangyuan Elementary has used HABOOK's systems since 2010, one of the platform's longest-running users, driven by a consistent interest in reducing teacher workload while raising outcomes.

Zhuo Meiyue at Taipei's Penglai Elementary treats teaching as a vocation in the fullest sense — nearly three decades across homeroom, administration and specialist roles, still describing herself as committed to passing knowledge and experience forward. Lin Yujun, a Taoyuan education-bureau specialist, pairs team-based learning with smart-classroom tools and reading-comprehension strategy to help students genuinely enjoy math rather than merely tolerate it.

Xu Jingyi at Taipei Municipal University of Education's affiliated elementary — one-third of the WRITE writing-model team from our May News Digest — demonstrates how the same smart-classroom tools support the Ministry's competency-based curriculum, building self-directed learners through structured strategy and reflection. Zeng Jingyi, principal of Taoyuan's Zhongxing Elementary — the hundred-year-old school we mentioned back in January — brings a counseling-psychology background to the job, a national SUPER Teacher award and a Ministry Shi Duo Award of her own, and a stated goal of making sure every student at her school is genuinely seen.

Dai Yanru at Taipei's Jiancheng Junior High frames digital learning as tool, strategy and content all at once, aiming to prepare students for whatever comes next rather than for any one test. Professor Zeng Suqiu at National Chiayi University applies HiTeach across both her own teaching and her work training future teachers, using it for interactive instruction, assessment and classroom observation alike.

Hua Meizhen at Taipei's Mingde Elementary belongs to Professor Chen Xinxi's ten-school reading alliance, where lessons are recorded and archived so teachers across schools can study each other's technique frame by frame. Chen Yingru at Taipei's Wanhua Junior High ran a public history lesson on ancient Greece with one tablet per student, a fully worked example of technology, pedagogy and content fused into a single observable class. Co-founder and head of R&D Liang Renkai — whose live probability lesson we covered in June — holds a doctorate in computer science and has led HABOOK's hardware and software development, including radio-frequency and assessment-analytics work, since the company's early days. And we close with Zhuang Yaling at Taoyuan Elementary, whose science lessons increasingly stretch learning beyond the classroom's four walls.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 9 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 10',
      scope:'PAGE 10 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Ten of Featured Educators.

Lai Yingwei at Taoyuan's Qingpu Junior High teaches biology around a simple principle: attention starts with interest, so a strong hook matters as much as the content that follows — data and technology then help confirm whether the foundation actually holds before he moves the class forward. Lin Lingyi at a Taoyuan elementary school builds math lessons around real problem-solving and team-based learning, pairing hands-on manipulatives with technology to give students what she calls genuine "math happiness."

Huang Chengwen at Taoyuan's Wenhua Elementary has spent nearly a decade making English feel usable rather than academic, building lessons around games and real-life scenarios. Huang Xinyao at Taoyuan's Yangmei Junior High, a visual-arts graduate, has watched smart-classroom tools pull reluctant students back into art class by blending new technology with traditional practice.

Professor Zhang Yihua at National Chengchi University — whose principal-focused talk we covered back in our July News Digest — researches school technology leadership and smart-education practice, and has published a body of work spanning multiple peer-reviewed journals and conference papers. Professor Chen Xinxi, introduced back in Volume One, gets a fuller picture here: founder of the "Little Owl" smart-alliance network, running reading-promotion and talent-development programs while helping teachers and principals nationwide co-plan, observe and discuss lessons online together.

You Shuohuan at Taipei's Jinhua Junior High runs an English unit where students become travel planners, presenting Taiwan's most striking destinations to the world in English. Pan Junhong, a student-affairs director at Taoyuan's Jingguo Junior High, tackled a famously abstract physics topic — pressure — by designing hands-on lab equipment and using spreadsheets to visualize the underlying proportional relationships. Dr. Ge Yan, a senior teacher at Singapore's Fuhua Primary School, opens lessons with movies students already love — Beauty and the Beast, Frozen, Kung Fu Panda — to teach story structure, then uses classroom technology to turn it into a shared discussion for the whole class.

Professor Xu Yujian at National Taipei University of Education, who also leads the Ministry's Chinese-language teacher-training research center, focuses on reading instruction and assessment design, and has spent years developing a "smart reading" teaching model meant to make both teaching and learning easier and more effective. Xie Wanyun at Taipei's Penglai Elementary demonstrated a full public lesson as part of a global同步 teaching showcase, with observers reviewing the recorded footage and discussion notes afterward. And we close with Zhang Mingzhong, also at Penglai Elementary, whose health-education classes pair student-centered strategy with real-time assessment and two-way feedback — part of the same global showcase lesson series.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 10 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 11',
      scope:'PAGE 11 OF 12 · 12 PROFILES',
      script:`Volume Eleven of Featured Educators — a batch anchored by two schools' worth of teachers who all took part in the same global showcase lesson event, plus a trio visiting from a partner school in Chengdu.

Wang Wenqing at Taipei's Penglai Elementary runs student-centered, small-group learning with differentiated tasks matched to student ability, part of the same 2020 global showcase-teaching event we heard about from her colleagues in Volume Ten. Wu Mingyong at Taipei's Muzha Elementary has loved math since childhood and teaches toward one consistent goal: getting students to use math to actually think through and solve problems in daily life. Liu Jiayun, also at Muzha Elementary, used that same showcase lesson as a chance to trade notes with teaching partners from other countries and regions, bringing new techniques back to her own classroom.

Three teachers from Chengdu's Xichuan Experimental School round out an international thread. Zheng Weitai teaches probability through hands-on group work rather than lecture, building intuition for uncertainty as something genuinely useful in daily decision-making. Xiong Lijie, an English teacher there, holds both senior-high English and international Chinese-teaching credentials, and has been recognized in national language-education research circles for her published case studies. Li Wenxian, in the same school's language department, describes teaching as "dialogue across time and space" — using technology to make deeper thinking and more concrete emotional expression possible in the classroom.

You Shumin at Taoyuan's Guangming Junior High — the school anchoring our Research Spotlight episodes — teaches geography around a simple hope: that students leave her class able to imagine their own future, grounded in real-world experience. Yi Xinying, also at Guangming, teaches music through team-based inquiry and discussion, keeping lessons lively through genuine questioning rather than lecture. Zhou Yijin, teaching English at the same school, replaces one-way instruction with real back-and-forth, using team-based learning and smart-classroom tools to put students in charge of their own classroom.

We close with three teachers from Taipei's Penglai Elementary: Li Qiongyao, who scaffolds differently for different ability levels — support early, collaboration mid-lesson, extension challenges at the end — within the very same class; Chen Yahui, a veteran math teacher who turns lessons into games and competitions so students learn from and with each other; and Chen Yanxiu, a longtime homeroom teacher who treats every classroom activity as a chance for genuine reflection, not just an assignment to complete.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 11 頁'}]
    },
    {
      title:'Volume 12',
      scope:'PAGE 12 OF 12 · 1 PROFILE',
      script:`Volume Twelve — the last stop in HABOOK's Smart Teacher directory, and it's a short one: just a single profile, closing out a hundred-and-thirty-five educators across twelve volumes.

Professor Chen Baoshan teaches in the education department at Chinese Culture University, where he uses HiTeach Mobile paired with the student-facing HiLearning Mobile app to let students take part in peer review and give classroom feedback directly from their phones — a simple, low-friction way to keep a college lecture hall genuinely interactive, and to keep students paying attention because they know they'll be asked to weigh in.

That's the last entry in the directory as it stands — from a "Tomato Teacher" in Taoyuan all the way to a university lecture hall in Taipei, twelve volumes covering elementary classrooms, junior highs, senior highs, universities, two countries beyond Taiwan, and just about every subject taught in a K-12 school. If there's one thread running through all hundred and thirty-five of them, it's this: the technology shows up differently in every single classroom, but the reason for using it barely changes from one teacher to the next — see the student in front of you a little more clearly, and give them one more reason to want to be in the room.`,
      sources:[{date:'directory', zh:'成功案例・智慧名師（講師人才庫）第 12 頁'}]
    }
  ]
}

